IMPORT prelude

-- Legislation Act 2001 (Australian Capital Territory): the cross-references of the in-scope text, one row per reference.
--
-- A script reads the text file, finds every citation of a section, chapter, part, division, schedule, table or the dictionary in the in-scope text,
-- and writes one row for it.
-- A reference to another Act or instrument is a row of its own kind: it carries the instrument and the provision as printed and is not resolved here.
-- A reference to a provision that is out of scope is a row whose target is marked `out of scope`.
-- `words as printed` is the text of the citation; `heading printed with the citation` is the bracketed heading that follows it, if there is one.
-- The text is the unofficial plain-text rendering deposited with this encoding, not the authorised republication.

§ `Cross-references`

DECLARE `Kind of reference` IS ONE OF
    `to a section`
    `to a chapter`
    `to a part`
    `to a division`
    `to a schedule`
    `to a part of the dictionary`
    `to the dictionary`
    `to a table`
    `to a provision of the section or definition it is in`
    `to another Act or instrument`

DECLARE `Where the target is` IS ONE OF
    `in scope`
    `out of scope`
    `not resolved here`

DECLARE `Cross-reference` HAS
    `row` IS A NUMBER
    `line in the text` IS A NUMBER
    `citing provision` IS A STRING
    `words as printed` IS A STRING
    `kind` IS A `Kind of reference`
    `cited identifier` IS A STRING
    `pinpoint path` IS A STRING
    `heading printed with the citation` IS A STRING
    `heading of the cited provision` IS A STRING
    `target` IS A `Where the target is`


GIVETH A LIST OF `Cross-reference`
`the cross-references` MEANS
